WEBVTT

00:00.574 --> 00:03.480
- [Instructor] Consider the
following piecewise function

00:03.480 --> 00:05.918
and we say f(t) is equal to

00:05.918 --> 00:07.515
and they tell us what it's equal to

00:07.515 --> 00:09.401
based on what t is,

00:09.401 --> 00:10.924
so if t is less than or equal to -10,

00:10.924 --> 00:11.963
we use this case.

00:11.963 --> 00:13.969
If t is between -10 and -2,

00:13.969 --> 00:15.006
we use this case.

00:15.006 --> 00:17.083
And if t is greater than or equal to -2,

00:17.083 --> 00:18.737
we use this case.

00:18.737 --> 00:19.570
And then they ask us

00:19.570 --> 00:21.809
what is the value of f(-10)?

00:21.809 --> 00:23.766
So t is going to be equal to -10,

00:23.766 --> 00:25.810
so which case do we use?

00:25.810 --> 00:26.643
So let's see.

00:26.643 --> 00:29.345
If t is less than or equal to -10,

00:29.345 --> 00:31.374
we use this top case, right over here

00:31.374 --> 00:33.218
and t is equal to -10,

00:33.218 --> 00:34.839
that's the one that
we're trying to evaluate.

00:34.839 --> 00:38.334
So we wanna use this case right over here.

00:38.334 --> 00:42.501
So f(-10) is going to be equal to -10,

00:44.289 --> 00:45.674
everywhere we see a t here,

00:45.674 --> 00:48.267
we substitute it with a -10.

00:48.267 --> 00:50.434
- 10 squared minus 5 times,

00:51.396 --> 00:52.942
actually I don't have a denominator there,

00:52.942 --> 00:54.498
I don't know why I wrote it so high.

00:54.498 --> 00:58.415
So it's gonna be -10
squared minus 5 times -10.

01:00.498 --> 01:01.331
So let's see.

01:01.331 --> 01:04.257
- 10 squared, that's positive 100

01:04.257 --> 01:05.295
and then negative,

01:05.295 --> 01:07.545
or subtracting 5 times -10,

01:08.746 --> 01:11.376
this is going to be subtracting -50

01:11.376 --> 01:13.575
or you're going to add 50,

01:13.575 --> 01:16.232
so this is going to be equal to 150.

01:16.232 --> 01:18.942
f(10) is 150,

01:18.942 --> 01:20.317
'cause we used this case up here,

01:20.317 --> 01:21.650
'cause t is -10.

01:22.769 --> 01:25.174
Let's do another one of these examples.

01:25.174 --> 01:28.115
So, here we have

01:28.115 --> 01:30.592
consider the following
piecewise function, alright.

01:30.592 --> 01:32.944
What is the value of h(-3)?

01:32.944 --> 01:34.455
See when h is -3,

01:34.455 --> 01:36.304
which case do we use?

01:36.304 --> 01:41.175
We use this case if our x
is between negative infinity

01:41.175 --> 01:42.643
and zero.

01:42.643 --> 01:46.288
And -3 is in between
negative infinity and zero,

01:46.288 --> 01:48.783
so we're gonna use this
case right over here.

01:48.783 --> 01:50.153
If it was positive three,

01:50.153 --> 01:51.525
we would use this case.

01:51.525 --> 01:52.602
If it was positive 30,

01:52.602 --> 01:54.228
we would use this case.

01:54.228 --> 01:57.124
So we're going to use the first case again

01:57.124 --> 01:59.957
and so for h(-3),

02:00.794 --> 02:03.018
we're gonna take -3 to the third power.

02:03.018 --> 02:03.851
So let's see.

02:03.851 --> 02:08.445
h(-3) is going to be -3 to the third power

02:08.445 --> 02:09.528
which is -27.

02:11.828 --> 02:12.661
And we're done.

02:12.661 --> 02:14.602
That's h(-3).

02:14.602 --> 02:15.599
Because we're using this case,

02:15.599 --> 02:17.779
you could almost ignore
these second two cases

02:17.779 --> 02:19.161
right over here.

02:19.161 --> 02:20.805
Let's do one more example.

02:20.805 --> 02:22.981
This one's a little bit different.

02:22.981 --> 02:26.819
Below is a graph of the step function g(x)

02:26.819 --> 02:28.964
so we can see g(x) right over here.

02:28.964 --> 02:31.504
It starts when x equals -9, it's at 3,

02:31.504 --> 02:32.342
and then it jumps up,

02:32.342 --> 02:33.667
and then it jumps down.

02:33.667 --> 02:36.409
Match each expression with its value.

02:36.409 --> 02:38.992
So g(-3.0001),

02:40.436 --> 02:42.936
so -3.0001,

02:44.017 --> 02:45.854
so that's right over here

02:45.854 --> 02:47.177
and g of that,

02:47.177 --> 02:48.927
we see is equal to 3.

02:50.251 --> 02:54.073
So this is going to be
equal to 3 right over here.

02:54.073 --> 02:57.113
g(3.99999)

02:57.113 --> 03:01.676
3.99999, almost 4,

03:01.676 --> 03:04.473
so let's draw a dotted line right here,

03:04.473 --> 03:06.312
it's gonna be almost 4,

03:06.312 --> 03:10.394
well g(3.99999) is going to be 7.

03:10.394 --> 03:12.417
We see that right over there.

03:12.417 --> 03:14.437
So that is equal to 7.

03:14.437 --> 03:17.303
g(4.00001).

03:17.303 --> 03:19.887
So g(4) is still 7,

03:19.887 --> 03:22.656
but as soon as we go above 4,

03:22.656 --> 03:24.114
we drop down over here,

03:24.114 --> 03:28.197
so g(4.00001) is going to be -3.

03:29.961 --> 03:32.402
I wanna, actually, let's focus
on that a little bit more.

03:32.402 --> 03:33.922
How did I know that?

03:33.922 --> 03:37.303
Well I know that g(4) is 7 and not -3

03:37.303 --> 03:40.280
because we have this dot
is circled in up here

03:40.280 --> 03:41.919
and it's hollow down here.

03:41.919 --> 03:44.873
But as soon as we get
any amount larger than 4,

03:44.873 --> 03:46.854
then the function drops down to this.

03:46.854 --> 03:49.183
So 4.0000, as many,

03:49.183 --> 03:50.690
just slightly above 4,

03:50.690 --> 03:53.844
the value of our function
is going to be -3.

03:53.844 --> 03:55.991
Now let's do g(9).

03:55.991 --> 03:58.287
So g(9), that's when x is 9

03:58.287 --> 03:59.423
and we go down here.

03:59.423 --> 04:01.289
You might be tempted to say it's -3,

04:01.289 --> 04:03.036
but you see, at this
point right over here,

04:03.036 --> 04:05.058
we have an open circle.

04:05.058 --> 04:06.792
So that means that while it's not,

04:06.792 --> 04:10.147
you can't say that the
function is -3 right over there

04:10.147 --> 04:12.779
and there's no other place
where we have a filled-in circle

04:12.779 --> 04:14.549
for x equals 9

04:14.549 --> 04:18.895
so the function g actually
isn't defined at x equals 9.

04:18.895 --> 04:22.645
So I'm gonna put undefined
right over there.
